WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:09.423
By 1972, factories across Chile sent their numbers to Cybersyn's team in Santiago by telex, once a day.

00:00:11.123 --> 00:00:16.735
But one factory's number means nothing beside another's, unless both are measured the same way.

00:00:17.435 --> 00:00:24.667
Count workers absent one way in one factory and another way in the next, and a factory in trouble could look like the rest.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:32.067
Santiago needed every factory's words to mean the same, or it couldn't compare them, or add them up.

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:40.994
So in every factory's model, social unease would be measured one way: the share of the workers on its payroll absent that day.

00:00:42.694 --> 00:00:50.043
And the operations room would show every factory with the same icons, so anyone could read them without training in science.

00:00:51.543 --> 00:00:59.660
After the strike of October 1972, numbers from across the country went into one report, written every day at CORFO.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:09.557
Taken by hand to the presidential palace, it showed national production and the points of crisis, from numbers a few days old, not six months.

00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:18.052
At the utility, a new team building the home energy app opens a change: its first table, called customer.

00:01:18.752 --> 00:01:24.185
Customer already means three things here. This would be a fourth: whoever signs in to the app.

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:30.340
The review catches it before it reaches a table, from what the data architecture keeps.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:44.352
The app's table holds everyone who signs in: an account holder, but also a partner, a tenant, a grown-up child.

00:01:45.852 --> 00:01:50.102
Called just customer, anyone reading it would take it for one of the other three.

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:57.567
The last time one thing had six names, the January storm's texts missed 180 households.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:04.800
So the review flags it: customer has three meanings here, and a table can't be called just that.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:14.193
It reads what the data architecture keeps in the repository, with the map: names, a reference model, principles and decisions.

00:02:16.893 --> 00:02:22.110
Each name is built from parts, as an international standard for naming data suggests.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.166
First, the thing it's about, in the glossary's words: account holder.

00:02:28.266 --> 00:02:29.924
Then, what about it: start.

00:02:31.224 --> 00:02:35.820
Last, the kind of value, from a short list: a date. Account holder start date.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.340
As with social unease, each term is defined once, and every system uses it the same way.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.375
A word with more than one meaning here, like customer, is never a name on its own.

00:02:50.675 --> 00:02:57.328
Older systems keep their own names, each mapped once to the glossary's: billing's premise is a connection point.

00:03:00.428 --> 00:03:08.541
For much of this, the industry already has words: the Common Information Model, a reference model for electricity, from the IEC.

00:03:10.441 --> 00:03:15.973
It calls the point where power is delivered a usage point, and the premises a service location.

00:03:17.473 --> 00:03:22.019
Whoever receives the service is a customer, with a customer account for billing.

00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:31.829
The utility keeps its own words, and maps each to the model: a connection point to a usage point, an account holder to a customer.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:39.901
So a new system or a partner can be matched to the utility's words through words they already know.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:48.117
Whoever signs in to the app is none of the three: a new concept, which needs its own name and an owner.

00:03:51.417 --> 00:03:56.569
Then, a few principles: each a short rule, with why it holds and what follows from it.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:05.723
One concept, one name, from the glossary: six names for one thing left 180 households without a text.

00:04:07.223 --> 00:04:10.770
Each fact has one home, and every copy traces back to it.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:16.792
Data crosses a domain's edge only by a published flow, with its contract.

00:04:18.092 --> 00:04:23.429
And every table belongs to a data product, owned by the business that creates its data.

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:33.515
Each decision is written down beside the map: what was decided and why, what else was considered, who decided, and when.

00:04:35.015 --> 00:04:43.307
Decision seven: customer is never a name on its own. Grace, the head of retail and Ama decided it on 14 October.

00:04:45.007 --> 00:04:50.451
Why: here customer means three things, and a table called just that would be read as any of them.

00:04:51.151 --> 00:04:57.034
Considered, and turned down: one definition of customer for everyone. It would fit neither side.

00:04:58.334 --> 00:05:03.285
What follows: every name says which: account holder, connection point or contact.

00:05:04.785 --> 00:05:10.321
A record outlives the meeting and the people in it: the next team can read why, not only what.

00:05:13.021 --> 00:05:17.641
All of it is kept with the map, and every change is checked against it before it's made.

00:05:19.341 --> 00:05:24.776
The data team reviews the app team's change. What a new term means isn't theirs to decide.

00:05:26.676 --> 00:05:33.108
So the app team asks retail, in a ticket, and the head of retail defines the term in the glossary first.

00:05:34.808 --> 00:05:38.110
App user: a person who signs in to the app for an account.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:47.174
Then the table is built from it: app user, with the account each one acts for. The check passes, and so does the review.

00:05:49.274 --> 00:05:54.989
On the map, app user joins retail's concepts, with its owner and a link to decision seven.

00:06:01.489 --> 00:06:09.614
By May 1973, Cybersyn reached a quarter of the nationalised industries, more than a hundred, with half their revenue.

00:06:11.114 --> 00:06:18.902
It never ran as designed. The operations room could do very little, and the simulator never left the experimental stage.

00:06:20.202 --> 00:06:22.735
Only the telex network was used regularly.

00:06:23.435 --> 00:06:28.193
Beer later wrote that the model couldn't adequately represent the changes of those years.

00:06:29.893 --> 00:06:38.950
On 11 September 1973, a military coup ended Allende's government. Later, the operations room was dismantled.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:48.108
On the day of the coup, members of the team carried copies of its documents out of CORFO, to keep them for the future.

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:53.187
The ideas held. The practice never had time to mature.

00:06:55.287 --> 00:07:01.107
At the utility, the data architecture is never finished either: it matures one change at a time.

00:07:02.407 --> 00:07:08.941
In its first year, the review stopped nine names before they reached a table, and fourteen decisions were written down.
